PASS level_intents: all 66 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 6.8/10 (bar 8), 14 level(s) below 6/10: Well-structured, honest and readable curriculum with clear aha moments and a good breather rhythm. Main flaws: mid-skill win rates fall to 54-73% from Ch5 on, even on teach units; the defence-stand template repeats about 17 times; some units are broken or too narrow (47, 57, 61, 62); and the auction and ghost PvP are thin relative to the genre's retention drivers. Curve: Strong skeleton: Ch1 teaches one verb per unit, Ch2 adds the low moves, bonus and empty-raid/Do-or-Die, Ch3 adds Kick, Rush and scouting, Ch4 adds squad-building, Ch5 adds chain and formation themes, Ch6 adds fatigue, captains and feints. Breathers (21, 32, 43, 54, 63) sit after spikes. Problems: (1) Difficulty is inverted relative to the "teach" labels. Mid-skill win rates fall from about 90% in Ch1 to 54-73% in Ch5-6, and the competent-player wins on bosses drop to 29-33/40. Late chapters have many units at 60-70% mid-skill and below 75% casual. Several of those are teach units (45, 46, 47, 48, 51, 56, 57), which makes them punishing lessons. The curve needs a retune so that teach units are about 85%+ and only spikes and bosses dip. (2) Defence-teach units pile up. L4, 6, 15, 19, 25, 26, 29, 35, 36, 37, 39, 46, 47, 51, 58, 59 and 63 are all the same "stop N of 5 against a telegraph" skeleton, and many are Easy raiders. The defence loop is a read-and-tap mini-game with few levers, so repetition risk is high. (3) The Ch4 auction is only 3-4 units, is mostly a rigged budget puzzle, and has no ongoing economy in the content list. Auction, upgrades and Kit Album retention hooks aren't tested here. (4) Ghost PvP, the headline feature, is mostly scripted PvE (21, 32, 54 are score chases). (5) Final-boss units show 60-62% mid-skill and under 55% casual on L65, which is too punishing. (6) Chapter-boss pacing is fine, but the two-lesson and three-lesson stacks in Ch5-6 (L60, L62, L66) are overloaded. Positives: the READ-row transparency, the non-hidden-trap policy and consistent aha lines are excellent. Overall it is a well-designed teaching curriculum that over-corrects into difficulty and defence repetition late.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L47 (3/10): Casual 63%, skilled 65% (skill barely helps); skilled players fail a third; compound of many risks, goal confusing → Reduce raider skill, 4 raids, show chain 55%+.
L51 (4/10): Competent 27/40, casual 59%; plan is known yet player fails — plan-solving should be near guaranteed → Raise matching-counter bonus to +30 and lower grip.
L57 (4/10): Competent 28/40, mid 54%; five-man mat, scouting 9 pts, bonus dead, high held rates (30-50%); 4 points in 5 raids is too much → Target 3 points or reduce held rates and drop scouting.
L62 (4/10): Dash is 90% held and walk 61%, dubki scouted: all ways home bad; too many reads, mid 66% → Drop scouting or give one clearly good way home.
L19 (5/10): Mid-skill 66%, competent 35/40; lesson 'defence rebuilds' is hidden by high raider skill 0.54 with 83% touch → Lower raider skill to ~0.3 and raise stops; add start with 6 on mat.
L29 (5/10): Competent 28/40, a teach unit fails 30%; Kick trap makes defence feel unfair; 'Hold' never stops touches → Raise Hold effectiveness, lower raider skill to 0.4, give a 3-raid stop target.
L33 (5/10): Competent 30/40 and mid 60%; grip 1.14 and rival is a state champion with 4 touch types; punishing → Start 11-12, reduce grip to 0.9, drop scouting.
L44 (5/10): Mid 61%, competent 29/40, casual 70%; optional auction hard to value; grip 1.31 is grindy → Make auction buys visibly stronger and reduce grip to 1.0.
L46 (5/10): Casual 67%; chain 45-50% only slightly better than hold, so chain feels pointless → Boost chain to 65-70% in the lesson.
L49 (5/10): Mid 65%, casual 62% vs skilled 71%; bonus is 15% held but must be first; confusing 'twist' with scouting → Remove scouting; show bonus as safe.
L50 (5/10): DRS forced wrong calls ('wrong until first use') feels rigged, mid-skill 70% → Make DRS about information and give 2 reviews.
L58 (5/10): Competent 29/40, casual 61%; stop 3 of 6 vs ankle 36% only → Boost ankle on dubki to 55% or stop 2.
L60 (5/10): Stacks many levers, casual 62%; 16-18 down → Split reversal and fatigue into separate trials.
L65 (5/10): Casual 55%, mid 60%; Kick lands 2/3; too many variables → Reduce grip; start 18-18.
L6 (6/10): Near duplicate of L4 with one new rule; mid-skill 90% and raider is Easy → Merge cover-ring into L4 or make raider alternate touch sides so cover choice matters.
L14 (6/10): Mid-skill 78%; dash gem shown at 69% held as an obvious bad choice; goal 'lowMove' is vague → Show Dubki as only fair way home; make T corners not 32% held so the lesson is the way home only.
L16 (6/10): Too many lessons; 3-star 'exactly one empty raid' conflicts with touching risk on all-hard mat; best 2★ in proof → Fix a reachable 3★; reduce touch risk to 15-25% or lead more.
L25 (6/10): Casual 74%; three different reads; Rush is worst vs touch so mostly a trap → Make raider hop 70% and show hop telegraph clearly.
L27 (6/10): Casual 73%, mid 71%; scouting is only 7 points, nearly invisible to player; Kick requirement narrow → Use 10 pts and a prominent tag; drop Kick requirement.
L28 (6/10): Casual 67%; 4 raids, mat is odd, ordering puzzle unclear; 'never let lone survivor revive' is confusing → Clarify with a hint on remaining men and give 5 raids.
L30 (6/10): Mid 66%; one-plan raider is trivial but raid mat is harsh → Lower grip to 0.65 and add a revive.
L31 (6/10): Starting 3 on mat, 5-9: heavy; revival lesson overlaps 19 → Cut scouting; clarify one lever.
L35 (6/10): Casual 73%, competent 31/40 for a teach; the specialists only 1.25x, ankle/thigh read is repeat of L4 → Make specialists ×1.5 and use a raider with a fixed split.
L38 (6/10): Labelled practise but mid 67%; Hard CPU and grip .98 → Lower grip, make it a win by 1 clearly.
L40 (6/10): Mid 71%, competent 33/40; reads-your-shape Medium AI is invisible → Surface the AI's read as a tag.

level-design score 6.8/10 — Well-structured, honest and readable curriculum with clear aha moments and a good breather rhythm. Main flaws: mid-skill win rates fall to 54-73% from Ch5 on, even on teach units; the defence-stand template repeats about 17 times; some units are broken or too narrow (47, 57, 61, 62); and the auction and ghost PvP are thin relative to the genre's retention drivers.
curve: Strong skeleton: Ch1 teaches one verb per unit, Ch2 adds the low moves, bonus and empty-raid/Do-or-Die, Ch3 adds Kick, Rush and scouting, Ch4 adds squad-building, Ch5 adds chain and formation themes, Ch6 adds fatigue, captains and feints. Breathers (21, 32, 43, 54, 63) sit after spikes. Problems: (1) Difficulty is inverted relative to the "teach" labels. Mid-skill win rates fall from about 90% in Ch1 to 54-73% in Ch5-6, and the competent-player wins on bosses drop to 29-33/40. Late chapters have many units at 60-70% mid-skill and below 75% casual. Several of those are teach units (45, 46, 47, 48, 51, 56, 57), which makes them punishing lessons. The curve needs a retune so that teach units are about 85%+ and only spikes and bosses dip. (2) Defence-teach units pile up. L4, 6, 15, 19, 25, 26, 29, 35, 36, 37, 39, 46, 47, 51, 58, 59 and 63 are all the same "stop N of 5 against a telegraph" skeleton, and many are Easy raiders. The defence loop is a read-and-tap mini-game with few levers, so repetition risk is high. (3) The Ch4 auction is only 3-4 units, is mostly a rigged budget puzzle, and has no ongoing economy in the content list. Auction, upgrades and Kit Album retention hooks aren't tested here. (4) Ghost PvP, the headline feature, is mostly scripted PvE (21, 32, 54 are score chases). (5) Final-boss units show 60-62% mid-skill and under 55% casual on L65, which is too punishing. (6) Chapter-boss pacing is fine, but the two-lesson and three-lesson stacks in Ch5-6 (L60, L62, L66) are overloaded. Positives: the READ-row transparency, the non-hidden-trap policy and consistent aha lines are excellent. Overall it is a well-designed teaching curriculum that over-corrects into difficulty and defence repetition late.
